    "created_at": "2025-09-18T09:39:24Z",
    "updated_at": "2025-09-18T09:39:24Z",
    "body": "Worth noting that the _extremely_ popular `ws` package (now 110 million downloads a week - more than `react` + `express` combined!) supports 3 arguments ([docs](https://github.com/websockets/ws/blob/master/doc/ws.md#new-websocketaddress-protocols-options)) as does `faye-websocket` (20 million/week, [docs](https://www.npmjs.com/package/faye-websocket#initialization-options)). In this case the protocol is optional instead of just overloading the 2nd arg:\n\n```js\nnew WebSocket(address[, protocols][, options])\n```\n\nIn that case, `protocol` isn't included as a valid option in `options` - if you want to specify subprotocols, you have to provide all 3 arguments. That protocols argument is optional though, so if you don't need it, you can use `new WebSocket(address, options)` with those libraries as well.\n\nThis API has the advantage of supporting out-of-the-box browser compatibility: `new WebSocket(url, { ... })` will throw in browsers due to the invalid argument, so any code using that API is not currently portable to browsers (and probably never will be), while `new WebSocket(url, null, { ... })` works fine in browsers today, with the options simply being ignored.\n\nStandardizing options as a 3rd argument would help to ensure browsers preserve that status quo, which means we'd have a single API offering easy compatibility everywhere, with minimal effort for everybody. Lovely.\n\nWould be nice to see some consensus on the different approaches here, since a consistent API would be very helpful. @KhafraDev @mcollina @uzlopak on the Undici side, would you be open to matching the `ws` API here, and starting to gently move away from your current websocket options API? Looks very easy to support both without any breaking changes. I'm not sure who to ping for Bun but hopefully they'd be open to following suit. CC @lpinca who might also have opinions here.",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
